## 3. Grain and key design

The **grain** is what one row represents. It must be documented before calculating totals or connecting fact tables.

| Table | Grain to confirm | Questions to validate |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `fact_sales` | Possibly one order line | Can an order have multiple lines? Is `line_total` additive? |
| `fact_inventory` | Possibly one product per month | Is `units` stock on hand (snapshot) or units moved (flow)? |
| `fact_campaign_spend` | Possibly one campaign per date | Are there multiple channels or records per day? |
| `fact_promotion_coverage` | Possibly one product–campaign assignment | Are `(product_key, campaign_key)` pairs unique? |
| `fact_order_process` | Possibly one order | Is `order_id` unique? Are multiple invoices or payments possible? |
| `fact_sales_targets` | Possibly one target per date | Are targets daily, monthly, or allocated to a different level? |

Do not sum snapshot inventory across months as though it were transactional activity; consider an end-of-period measure once the grain is confirmed.

